window.docContents[40].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Transport-and-Characteristics":{"contents":"We follow Evans, §2.1. A constant velocity  determines\nthe spacetime direction , and the characteristic through  is\n. Integrating the source along this line gives the\nrepresentation formula; differentiating the formula recovers the equation.\n\n\n\nFor a constant spatial velocity , the transport operator is the\nFréchet derivative in spacetime direction . Its codomain is an\narbitrary real normed space, so the same declaration applies to scalar\nunknowns and systems.\n\nThe abstract directional derivative equals the familiar coordinate operator\n\nThe first identity is vector-valued; the second identifies the scalar spatial\nsum with .\n\nDecompose the spacetime direction  into its time basis vector and\nspatial coordinate directions. Linearity of the Fréchet derivative gives the\ntime partial plus the weighted spatial partials; in the scalar case the\nfinite sum is the Euclidean inner product with the gradient.\n\nThe operator by itself fixes only the equation; the Cauchy problem adds a\nprescribed value on the hyperplane .\n\nThe source-parametric initial-value problem consists of\n on  and . Homogeneous\ntransport is the specialization .\n\nThe initial-value problem is equivalent to two clauses: the transport\nresidual on positive time and the assigned value on .\n\nUnfold the initial-value predicate. Its two fields are precisely the\npositive-time residual equation and the assigned initial value, so projection proves\none direction and pairing the two clauses constructs the converse.\n\nLinearity gives the usual reduction of uniqueness to the homogeneous problem\nwith zero initial value.\n\nFix  and assume  and  are Fréchet differentiable at every\nspacetime point with . The residual is linear on positive time, and\ninitial evaluation respects the same combination:Consequently zero, addition, scalar multiplication, negation, subtraction,\nand arbitrary binary linear combinations preserve the corresponding source\nand assigned initial data. In particular, two sufficiently differentiable\nsolutions with the same source and assigned value have a difference\n satisfying  and .\n\nAt positive-time points, differentiability of the fields justifies applying\nthe Fréchet derivative to their linear combination. Evaluation at the initial\nsurface respects the same combination, giving the corresponding sources and\nassigned values. Subtracting equal data gives the homogeneous zero-trace\nproblem. The zero and negation identities also hold without differentiability.\n\nThe first explicit solution concerns the homogeneous case . The field\n is constant along every line of direction , so its\ntime derivative exactly cancels the advection term.\n\nFor differentiable initial data, translation along  solves the\nhomogeneous specialization of , including\nits initial trace.\n\nThe proof differentiates the affine foot map, applies the chain rule to\n, and cancels the time derivative against the spatial advection.\n\nThe translation  in that solution is the point where the line of\ndirection  through  meets the initial hyperplane. Naming this\nline, with its parametrization by time, reduces the forced problem to an\nordinary differential equation along it.\n\nThe characteristic through  is\n. It joins the initial foot  to\n and has constant spacetime velocity .\n\nAlong , a differentiable profile obeysThe endpoint identities  and\n are part of the statement.\n\nThe affine characteristic has derivative . The chain rule evaluates\n on this direction, which is the transport operator by its coordinate\nidentity. Substituting  and  gives the two endpoints.\n\nFor a solution of the forced problem the chain rule along a characteristic\nreads . Integrating from\n, where the curve sits at the initial foot, to  produces the\nrepresentation formula, which is thus a consequence of the equation rather\nthan a separate ansatz.\n\nLet  satisfy the transport residual and initial-value clauses, and let\n. Assume  is Fréchet differentiable in spacetime at\n for every , including the endpoints, and\n is interval integrable on . Then\n\nThe continuous-source variant obtains these hypotheses from global\nFréchet differentiability of  and continuity of .\n\nAmbient differentiability of  along the closed characteristic segment\ngives continuity of its pullback on . On , the chain rule\nand the residual equation give derivative . Its interval\nintegrability permits the fundamental theorem of calculus; the endpoint\nidentities and assigned initial value then give the formula.\n\nThe right-hand side of the representation depends only on the data , ,\nand , not on the solution  it was derived from. Detaching it gives an\nexplicit spacetime field whose properties can be checked directly: that it\nsolves the problem for differentiable data, and that every sufficiently\nregular solution coincides with it.\n\nThe explicit spacetime function isPulling  back to a characteristic gives\nthe initial value plus the accumulated source, while the coordinate theorem\nseparates the footpoint  from time.\n\nFor differentiable  and a  source, the explicit formula is\ndifferentiable, has initial trace , and satisfies the forced transport\nequation. When the source is only continuous, a variant takes\ndifferentiability of the formula as a hypothesis and reaches the same\nconclusion.\n\nFirst write the formula in the adapted coordinates :\n. Since  is , the integrand\nand its derivative with respect to  are continuous. On a compact\nneighborhood of the parameter and time segment, this derivative is uniformly\nbounded, so the parametric integral rule proves spacetime differentiability.\nAdding differentiable  and composing with the linear coordinate map\npreserves it. Restricted to a characteristic, the formula has derivative equal\nto the source by the fundamental theorem of calculus. The characteristic\nchain rule then identifies that derivative with the transport operator, and\nsetting  gives the assigned initial value.\n\nUniqueness needs only the representation theorem: global differentiability\nlets it be applied along every characteristic at once.\n\nEvery globally differentiable solution with continuous source satisfies, for\n,Consequently, if  and  have the same , , and , then\n throughout the nonnegative-time domain.\n\nThe representation theorem identifies every solution with the explicit\nformula determined by , , and . Two solutions with the same data\nare therefore both equal to that formula and hence equal to each other.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves","header":"7.2.1. Transport and Characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Transport-and-Characteristics"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Identifying-the-Regular-Wave":{"contents":"The differentiated weak limits are derivatives of one solution, and elliptic\nlifting applies to its spatial slices. Here the time-regularity and\nspatial-regularity arguments meet.\n\nFor an autonomous operator, suppose the original limit is a weak wave, the\nforcing has a weak time derivative, and the original and differentiated\nGalerkin families have common weak limits with their exact derivative links.\nThe theorem identifies , , and a third weak time derivative.\nUniform essential bounds on the differentiated families pass to the limit.\n\nTest the Galerkin identity relating each trajectory to its time derivative\nagainst smooth compactly supported time functions. Weak convergence passes\nthat distributional identity to the paired limits, identifying the latter as\nsuccessive weak derivatives of one function. Then differentiate\n: autonomy gives\n in . The almost-everywhere norm-bound\nsets are closed and convex in , so their weak closedness preserves the\nuniform essential bounds.\n\nFor the pivot space , the paired-limit theorem identifies\nthe dual-valued acceleration with the pivot image of an -valued\nclass and preserves its essential spatial  bound. That is the input the\nelliptic lift needs at almost every time.\n\nLet  and let the domain carry the operational C2BoundaryAtlas.\nAssume uniformly elliptic  principal coefficients with global\n representatives. For a time- trajectory in \nsatisfying the weak wave equation with spatial  forcing and\nacceleration, essential bounds on those two spatial norms give an essentially\nbounded  lift. The elliptic solver's operator norm gives the quantitative\nestimate.\n\nFor almost every time, rewrite the wave equation as the elliptic problem\n. The global elliptic regularity solver supplies an\n representative of that spatial slice, and uniqueness identifies it\nwith . Measurability of the bounded solver assembles these slices into\na Bochner representative, while the operator norm and the two essential\n bounds give the essential-supremum estimate.\n\nAssembling the pieces gives the existence theorem. Symmetry of the spatial\noperator is assumed because the chain theorem invoked is the symmetric one.\n\nUnder the explicit compatibility, stable-density, coercivity, Ritz, and\nelliptic-lifting hypotheses, and assuming the spatial operator is symmetric,\nthere exists a weak wave with the prescribed initial data and an essentially\nbounded Sobolev time jet of order  whose head agrees almost everywhere with\nthe displacement and whose first time derivative is the velocity. For a nonsymmetric operator,\nthe corresponding steps are the\nnonsymmetric Galerkin chain\nand the higher-order lift.\n\nThe compatibility data initialize all differentiated Galerkin systems. The\nfinite common weak-wave chain supplies the time derivatives, and the assumed\nelliptic lift raises spatial order two derivatives at a time. Induction\nassembles the resulting representatives into one essentially bounded\nSobolev time jet.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Higher Regularity and Compatibility","header":"7.3.3.3. Identifying the Regular Wave","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Identifying-the-Regular-Wave"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure":{"contents":"Weak solutions of the fluid equations live on divergence-free fields, and the\nenergy estimates that control them come from a cancellation that holds only\nthere. The results specialize the weak-compactness theorems of the variational\nchapter and the Bochner constraint theorems of the parabolic chapter to a\nsingle linear constraint.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.3. Solenoidal Spaces, Compactness, and Pressure","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Integration/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature":{"contents":"A quadrature certificate bounds the difference between a proposed value and\nthe integral. Exactness on polynomials and bounds on derivatives control\nthe analytic error; interval evaluation controls the error in computing the\nfinite weighted sum. These two errors add.\n\nA quadrature certificate stores a proposed value of a Bochner interval\nintegral, an explicit error budget, and a proof of the corresponding norm\nestimate. The certificate is indexed by the ordinary Lean integrand and\nendpoints, so no symbolic expression type appears in theorem statements.Its trusted field is\n\nA complete certificate: Simpson on .\n\nTake  on . One Simpson panel uses the values at\n, , and , so its exact-real value is\n\n\n\nBecause Simpson's rule is exact for every polynomial of degree at most three,\nits quadrature certificate has:\n\n* the stored value is ;* the stored error is ;* soundness proves .\n\nThe third field is the mathematical content. The first two fields are data\nthat later planners can inspect and combine. Since the integrand is a\ncontinuous polynomial, it is also interval integrable; that fact is separate\nbecause QuadratureCert itself is stated for Mathlib's totalized interval\nintegral.\n\nFor a nonpolynomial integrand, the shape of the object is unchanged. A\nSimpson or Gauss–Legendre theorem supplies a positive analytic error, and a\nverified numerical backend may replace the exact-real weighted sum with a\ncomputed value by adding a separately proved roundoff error. The final\nsound field always states one inequality about the original integral.\n\nThe first polynomial beyond Simpson's exactness range shows what the error\nconstant measures. For  on ,\n\n\n\nHere , so the sharp Simpson bound is\n, exactly the observed error. The fourth derivative\nmeasures the first behavior that the degree-three exactness does not\nreproduce.\n\nIf both functions are interval integrable, uniform approximation on\n transports through interval integration with error\n. Consequently a quadrature certificate for a\nfinite or symbolic model becomes a certificate for the original integrand,\nwith model and quadrature errors added explicitly.\n\nIntegrate the pointwise difference and apply the norm bound for an interval\nintegral. Uniform approximation bounds the integrand norm by\n, while the integral of that constant over the oriented\ninterval contributes . A final triangle inequality adds this model\nerror to the existing quadrature error.\n\nIf the scalar integrand is interval integrable and one backend interval contains every value on\n, its midpoint defines a constant integral model and its radius gives\nthe uniform pointwise error. The resulting certificate has value\n and error , with no endpoint-order assumption and no\nbackend-specific integration theorem. Exact rational ranges, directed\nfloating-point ranges, Taylor enclosures, and adaptive local ranges therefore\nshare the same analytic bridge.\n\nWrite the enclosure as center  and radius . Interval membership gives\n uniformly, so integration stability compares  with the\nconstant function . The constant integral is , and its\ntransported error is .\n\nHow the error budget is assembled.\n\nA certificate constructor assembles the error budget in five steps:\n\n1. prove exactness on a polynomial model or prove a Peano-kernel remainder;2. obtain any required derivative bound as an ordinary theorem;3. sum local analytic errors when the interval is subdivided;4. enclose the finite weighted sum if it is evaluated numerically;5. add the proved roundoff radius to the analytic budget.\n\nThe quadrature rule chooses nodes and weights. The derivative theorem controls\ntruncation. The numerical backend controls finite evaluation. Keeping those\nproofs separate makes it possible to replace one backend or one subdivision\npolicy without changing the statement about the integral.\n\nQuadrature rules implemented.\n\nThe certificate constructors use four normalized rules, each stored as an\nexact list of (weight, node) pairs on . Each error formula assumes\nthe stated derivative bound and the corresponding  or \nregularity on an ordered interval .\n\n* Trapezoidal rule: two nodes at  with weights .\nExact for polynomials of degree at most 1. Error on :\n where  bounds .* Midpoint rule: one node at  with weight . Exact for polynomials\nof degree at most 1. Error on : , half the\ntrapezoid constant.* Simpson's rule: three nodes at  with weights\n. Exact for polynomials of degree at most 3. The sharp\nPeano-kernel error on :  where  bounds\n.* Two-point Gauss–Legendre: two nodes at\n with weights\n. With only two nodes it is exact for polynomials of degree\nat most 3, like Simpson's three-node rule. The sharp Peano-kernel error:\n, which is  of Simpson's error constant.\n\nThe Gauss–Legendre rule has irrational nodes (), which\nare represented exactly in the real-valued QuadratureRule. Numerical\nevaluation encloses the exact weighted terms by proved intervals. A\nRationalQuadratureRule, by contrast, has rational nodes.\n\nThe Peano kernel approach.\n\nThe sharp error constants (1/2880 for Simpson, 1/4320 for Gauss–Legendre) come\nfrom the Peano kernel method. For a rule  that is exact on polynomials of\ndegree at most  and an integrand , the integration\nerror equals\n\n\n\nwhere  is the Peano kernel of order : the functional\n applied to the truncated power\n. If  has constant sign, the integral is bounded\nby .\n\nExample: composite Simpson on .\n\nFirst plan the exact-real composite value to analytic tolerance\n. A computed replacement must also fit its evaluation error\nwithin the remaining budget before claiming this same total tolerance.\n\n1. Derivative bound. On , . Set .2. Error constant. .3. Panel planning. Solve :\n.\nThe planner rounds up to : exactly,\n.4. Certificate construction. Build 18 Simpson panels of width .\nSumming their sharp errors gives\n.5. Value computation. The panel-major schedule contains 54 weighted samples,\nwith  distinct nodes because adjacent endpoints coincide.\nA numerical backend must provide enclosures for those samples and their\nweighted sum; the analytic panel count alone supplies no evaluation precision.6. Final certificate. The QuadratureCert stores:* value: the midpoint of the weighted-sum enclosure,* error: the analytic Simpson error plus the weighted-sum roundoff radius,* sound: the proof that .\n\nThis final error is at most  only if the certified evaluation radius\nis at most . Otherwise, refine the evaluation or\nreplan the analytic mesh with a smaller reserved budget.\n\nAdaptive quadrature and the tree structure.\n\nComposite rules use a fixed number of equal panels, which may be inefficient\nfor integrands with localized difficulty (near-singularities, rapid\noscillation). The adaptive framework addresses this with a binary\nQuadratureTree whose leaves are local certificates and whose branches split\nan interval at a point. Because soundness depends only on the leaf\ncertificates and their composition, an adaptive algorithm may choose the\nsubdivision by any heuristic, including Richardson extrapolation, derivative\nestimates, or a priori knowledge of integrand behavior.\n\nA normalized quadrature rule is an ordered list of exact-real weights and\nnodes in . The order remains explicit for verified finite-precision\nevaluation. Generic definitions transport the rule affinely to any oriented\ninterval, while moment identities characterize exactness independently of a\nparticular numerical backend.\n\nThe rational algebraic fragment of the shared expression language has a\nfail-closed exact evaluator. Every successful result is proved equal to the\nexisting real expression semantics. A quadrature rule with rational weights\nand nodes can therefore sample a symbolic integrand exactly, preserve the\nordered rule schedule, and cast term-for-term to the existing transported\nQuadratureRule. Transcendental nodes, malformed literals, and sampled zero\ndenominators are rejected. Optional\nfinite-precision backends consume the exact table through the same interval\nand weighted-sum interfaces.At the expression boundary,For a successfully compiled rule, casting every rational term preserves the\nanalytic schedule and value:\n\nProve the rational evaluator correct by structural induction on the algebraic\nexpression, rejecting precisely the division cases with zero denominator.\nApply that equality at each rational quadrature node. Casting commutes with\nthe finite products and sum, so the compiled ordered table has the same real\nweighted value as the analytic rule.\n\nIf a normalized rule has the exact moments through degree , it exactly\nintegrates every real polynomial of degree at most . Affine transport\npreserves this conclusion on arbitrary oriented intervals. Both the three-node\nSimpson rule and the two-point Gauss–Legendre rule have the exact moments\nthrough degree three, hence exactly integrate every cubic and generate\nzero-error QuadratureCerts.\n\nExpand a polynomial of degree at most  in the monomial basis. Linearity of\nboth integration and the weighted rule reduces exactness to each monomial,\nwhere it is exactly the assumed moment identity. Affine substitution\ntransports the normalized-interval result to any oriented interval.\n\nIf two scalar integrands differ uniformly by  on\n, their values under a normalized rule differ by at most\n, where  is the sum of the absolute rule weights.\nThis estimate lets a rule that is exact on a nearby model be evaluated on the\noriginal integrand while accounting separately for integral-model error and\nsample-value perturbation.\n\nAt every node, the sample difference is at most . Apply the\ntriangle inequality to the weighted sum, bound each term by\n, and sum to obtain  on the normalized\ninterval. Affine transport contributes the factor .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Integration","header":"10.8.1. Certified Quadrature","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Integration/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Integration/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Gaussian-quadrature":{"contents":"The degree of exactness of a rule with  nodes can never reach .\nThe nodal polynomial  has degree , so\n has degree ; the rule returns ,\nwhile . Gauss's theorem says that degree  is\nreached, for one choice of nodes only.\n\nWhy must the nodes be the roots of ? Suppose a rule with nodes\n is exact to degree . For any  of degree below  the\nproduct  has degree below , and it vanishes at every node,\nso\n\n\n\nThus  is monic of degree  and orthogonal to all lower degrees,\nand uniqueness forces . Conversely, take the nodes to be the\nroots of  and the weights interpolatory. Any  of degree below\n divides as  with . The rule sees only\n, since  vanishes at the nodes, and it integrates  exactly;\nthe integral sees only  as well, since . So\n.\n\nLet  be continuous on  and strictly positive on ,\n, and let . There are  distinct nodes in  whose\ninterpolatory weights  are all strictly positive and\nfor whichAs a NodalRule, the rule is positive, has its nodes in , and is\nexact to degree .\n\nTake the nodes to be the roots of . The interpolatory rule on \ndistinct nodes reproduces every polynomial of degree below . For\n, divide by the monic nodal polynomial: the quotient term is\nannihilated by the rule, because the nodal polynomial vanishes at the nodes,\nand by the integral, by orthogonality; the remainder has degree below\n. For the weights, apply exactness to , which has degree\n: the rule returns , and the integral\n is positive.\n\nThe nodes determine the weights, and the weights are positive. This\npositivity will give convergence for continuous integrands.\n\nA Gaussian rule with  nodes for the weight  on  is a\nstructure GaussianRule a b w hw n, where hw records that  is\ncontinuous on . It holds nodes , weights\n, and four facts: the nodes are pairwise distinct, they lie\nin the open interval , the weights are strictly positive, andIts value on a function  is . For  the rule is\nempty and the exactness field says nothing, so the theorems about Gaussian\nrules assume .\n\nDistinct nodes give the Lagrange and Hermite interpolants behind the\ncharacterization and the remainder formula. Interior nodes let a bound\nthat holds on  be applied at every node, and positive weights turn such\na bound into the stability and convergence estimates.\n\nFor , a weight that is strictly positive on , and ,\ngaussianRule is the Gaussian rule of the existence theorem, with nodes at the\nroots of  and interpolatory weights. Every Gaussian rule with  is a\npositive NodalRule exact to degree . Exactness on the constant \nmakes its weights add up to , so each weight is at most\n.\n\nFor every Gaussian rule with  nodes for a continuous weight that is\nstrictly positive on , the nodal polynomial  is the\nmonic orthogonal polynomial  of , and the weight at  is the\ninterpolatory weight , the Christoffel number. A Gaussian\nrule is therefore unique up to the order of its nodes.\n\nThe nodal polynomial times any  of degree below  has degree below\n and vanishes at every node, so its integral is its rule value, zero.\nUniqueness of the monic orthogonal polynomial identifies the nodal\npolynomial. For the weights, apply exactness to , which has degree\n and takes the value  at  and  at the other nodes.\n\n\n\nThe figure shows both halves of the argument for  and the Legendre\nweight. On the left,  has degree four; the rule returns zero,\nand orthogonality makes the integral zero too. On the right,  has\ndegree six; the rule still returns zero, but the integral is\n. That positive number reappears as the constant in the\nerror formula.\n\nThe error of a Gauss rule is found by comparing  with its Hermite\ninterpolant , the polynomial of degree below  that matches  and\n at the  nodes. The rule is exact on  and cannot tell \nfrom , so the error is . The pointwise remainder is\nthe familiar one for interpolation, with every node counted twice:\n\n\n\nBecause , a mean-value argument for the integral pulls the\nderivative out at a single point.\n\nLet  be continuous on  and strictly positive on ,\n, let  be a Gaussian rule with  nodes, and let\n on . There is  withwhere  is the nodal polynomial, equal to  by the\ncharacterization. Here  is the iterated derivative within , so smoothness\nis needed only on the closed interval. If  on ,\nthe error is at most .\n\nFix  away from the nodes and choose  so that\n vanishes at . Then  has double zeros at the \nnodes and a simple zero at ,  zeros counted with multiplicity. A\ngeneralized Rolle theorem, proved for a chain of functions each the\nderivative of the previous one on the open interval, gives a zero \nof . Since  and , this is\nthe pointwise remainder. Multiply by  and integrate. The continuous\nfunction  takes values between its minimum and maximum on\n, so the integral of  lies between those extremes times\n, and the intermediate value theorem produces .\nFinally  by the characterization.\n\nFor  and the Legendre weight the constant is\n. Rescaled to an interval of length ,\nwhere  scales by , the formula gives error\n, the same constant as the sharp two-point\nGauss–Legendre certificate, which is proved by a Peano kernel. For  the\nconstant on\n is .\n\nThe first four Gauss–Legendre rules have closed forms in radicals. On\n the one-point rule is the midpoint rule with weight , the\ntwo-point rule has nodes  and weights , the three-point rule\nhas nodes  and weights , and the\nfour-point rule has nodes  with weights\n, the larger weight at the inner pair. The library stores\nthem in the normalized convention on , checks the moments degree by\ndegree, and then identifies each with the orthogonal-polynomial\nconstruction.\n\nThe normalized three- and four-point Gauss–Legendre rules, written in\nradicals, are exact through degrees five and seven. Transported to\n, the midpoint rule, the two-point rule, and these two rules are\nGaussian rules for the unit weight, and the nodal polynomial of the\n-point rule is the monic Legendre polynomial of degree , defined as\nthe monic orthogonal polynomial of that weight, for . For\n and any , every Gaussian rule for the unit weight on \nhas the transported two-point nodes and weights and the same value as the\ntwo-point QuadratureRule.\n\nExactness through degree  is a finite list of moment identities, each\nchecked by normal-form arithmetic using  or the\nsquares of the four-point radicals. Positivity of the weights and the ordering\nof the nodes are inequalities between radicals. The characterization theorem\nthen identifies each nodal polynomial with the monic Legendre polynomial,\nand for  it pins down the nodes, whose weights are then fixed by\nexactness.\n\nBeyond four points the nodes are no longer convenient radicals, and the\nlibrary certifies them instead. A sparse rational polynomial  with\n for , a condition checked in rational\narithmetic from the moments of , is the monic Legendre polynomial of\ndegree  as soon as it is monic of degree . Its roots are isolated by\ncertified brackets (root brackets): a rational\ninterval with a sign change at the ends and a derivative enclosure of fixed\nsign, so exactly one root lies inside. Ordered disjoint brackets inside\n then define a Gaussian rule at any order , with no stored table\nof nodes.\n\nLet  be a sparse rational polynomial and let  valid root brackets for\n be ordered, each strictly to the left of the next. The bracketed roots\nare strictly increasing and lie in their brackets. If  is monic of degree\n over , its nodal polynomial on these roots is  itself.\nIf moreover the brackets lie inside  and the rational moments\n vanish for , the roots are the nodes of a\nGaussian rule for the unit weight on , with the interpolatory\nweights.\n\nEach valid bracket contains a unique root, and the ordering of the brackets\norders the roots. A monic polynomial of degree  with  distinct roots\nis the product of the corresponding linear factors. The vanishing moments,\nextended linearly to every  of degree below , make the nodal\npolynomial orthogonal to lower degrees; the Gaussian exactness and weight\npositivity arguments then apply without change.\n\nThe weights of a certified rule are enclosed from the same brackets. At a\nsimple root  of  the Lagrange basis polynomial is\n, so the Christoffel number is\n\n\n\nSynthetic division writes the integral as a polynomial in  with\nrational coefficients, so the weight is a rational function of the single\nroot , and interval evaluation over the bracket encloses it.\n\nIf the nodal polynomial of a family of nodes is  and  is a root,\nthe interpolatory weight at  is , where\n is the rational polynomial integratedQuotient p with\n. For a valid bracket the rational\ninterval weightInterval p bracket contains this quotient at the bracketed\nroot, and each Christoffel number of the certified rule lies in the interval\ncomputed from its own bracket.\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomial at a node is the nodal polynomial divided by\n and by the nodal weight, which equals  for a simple root.\nThe division by  is carried out monomial by monomial through\n, and integrating the result term\nby term gives . The bracket's direction fixes the sign of\n on it, so dividing the numerator enclosure by the derivative enclosure\nis sound.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Integration\u0009Certified Quadrature","header":"10.8.1.3. Gaussian quadrature","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Integration/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Gaussian-quadrature"}});